<h1>Food Industry Conveyors: Food-Grade Conveyor Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Food-grade conveyors use stainless steel frames, <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/food-ingredients-packaging/packaging-food-contact-substances-fcs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA</a>-compliant contact surfaces, and washdown-rated components to meet <a href="https://www.ams.usda.gov/sites/default/files/media/Guidelines%20for%20the%20Evaluation%20and%20Certification%20of%20the%20Sanitary%20Design%20and%20Fabrication%20of%20Meat%20and%20Poultry%20Processing%20Equipment.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">USDA</a>, <a href="https://www.3-a.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">3-A SSI</a>, and <a href="https://www.bissc.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BISSC</a> sanitary design standards. These conveyors transport raw ingredients, work-in-process items, and finished food products through processing and packaging lines while preventing contamination and enabling thorough cleaning.</p>
<p>Food conveyor systems cost substantially more than standard industrial conveyors because every component (frame, belt, bearings, motors, fasteners) must resist corrosion from cleaning chemicals and eliminate bacterial harborage points. FDA's food CGMP rule, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/117.40" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 117.40</a>, requires equipment to be adequately cleanable and food-contact surfaces to be corrosion-resistant and nontoxic.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/food-flexlink-bread-loaf-conveyors.webp" width="960" height="600" alt="Bread loaves travel in single file on two white plastic chain conveyors with stainless steel frames and rod guide rails in a bakery." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Bread loaves on plastic chain conveyors with stainless steel frames and guide rails. Conveyors that carry unwrapped food are the ones that need food-contact belt materials and hygienic frame design. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.flexlink.com/en/home/industries/food" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FlexLink</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="standards-heading">USDA, 3-A SSI &amp; BISSC: What Each Standard Requires</h2>
<p>Three organizations define sanitary design standards that apply to food conveyor equipment. Each standard focuses on different aspects of food safety, and your facility's regulatory requirements determine which ones apply. Many food plants require compliance with multiple standards simultaneously.</p>
<h3>USDA Acceptance</h3>
<p>USDA equipment acceptance for meat and poultry is a voluntary, fee-based review run by the USDA AMS Dairy Grading Branch. It evaluates equipment against the NSF/ANSI/3-A 14159 standards. Part 3 of that series, 14159-3, covers mechanical belt conveyors with exposed product. FSIS ended its mandatory prior-approval program for plant equipment in 1997. AMS states that it does not require equipment to be presented for review, but FSIS inspectors can ask a plant to modify equipment that appears to violate the standards. Buyers request USDA-accepted equipment to reduce that risk.</p>
<p>As one example, Dorner describes its <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/sanitary-stainless-steel-conveyors/aquapruf-conveyors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AquaPruf conveyors</a> as BISSC and USDA certified, with tip-up tails and tool-less disassembly. Ask any supplier for the acceptance documentation that matches the exact configuration you are buying.</p>
<h3>3-A Sanitary Standards (3-A SSI)</h3>
<p>3-A Sanitary Standards Inc. publishes design criteria for equipment used in dairy, food, and beverage processing. 3-A criteria call for product-contact surfaces at least as smooth as 32 microinch (0.8 µm) Ra, radii of at least 1/4 inch on internal angles under 135 degrees, approved stainless materials, and self-draining design so no liquid pools during cleaning (<a href="https://my.3-a.org/Portals/93/Documents/Annual%20Meetings%20Presentations/May1_Basics_02_Hygienic%20Design%20Considerations%20and%20Techniques.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">3-A SSI hygienic design presentation</a>).</p>
<p>3-A compliance matters most in dairy processing, liquid food handling, and any application where the conveyor contacts pumpable food products. Product-contact surfaces must be accessible for cleaning and inspection, either in place (CIP) or after disassembly.</p>
<h3>BISSC (Baking Industry Sanitation Standards Committee)</h3>
<p>BISSC publishes the ANSI/BISSC/Z50.2 standard for bakery equipment and certifies equipment that meets it. These standards address flour dust accumulation, high-temperature zones near ovens, and the unique cleaning challenges of dough-contact surfaces. BISSC-compliant conveyors feature sealed frames that prevent flour ingress, smooth surfaces that resist dough adhesion, and construction that withstands the thermal cycling common in bakery environments.</p>
<h2 id="materials-heading">304 vs. 316 Stainless Steel: Choosing the Right Grade</h2>
<p>Stainless steel grade selection drives a significant portion of food conveyor cost. The two primary grades, 304 and 316, serve different applications based on your product chemistry and cleaning protocols. Choosing the wrong grade leads to premature corrosion, pitting, and eventual contamination risk.</p>
<h3>304 Stainless Steel (18/8)</h3>
<p>304 stainless steel is formulated at about 18% chromium and 8% nickel (<a href="https://www.foodprotection.org/files/food-protection-trends/Oct-12-Schmidt.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Schmidt et al., Food Protection Trends</a>). It resists corrosion from water, steam, mild acids, and most food products. 304 is the standard grade for food conveyors in dry food processing, bakery, snack food, confectionery, and general food packaging applications. It handles standard alkaline and quaternary ammonium sanitizers without degradation.</p>
<p><strong>Choose 304 when:</strong> your product is dry or low-acid, your cleaning chemistry uses standard quaternary or alkaline sanitizers, and your environment does not involve salt brines or chlorinated cleaners.</p>
<h3>316 Stainless Steel (Molybdenum-Bearing)</h3>
<p>316 stainless steel adds 2.0-3.0% molybdenum and raises nickel to 10-14% compared with 304. Molybdenum provides significantly better resistance to chloride-induced pitting and crevice corrosion. This matters in food environments with salt exposure, acidic products, or chlorine-based cleaning chemicals.</p>
<p><strong>Choose 316 when:</strong> you process meat, poultry, seafood, dairy, citrus, tomato products, pickled foods, or any product with significant salt or acid content. Also choose 316 if your sanitation program uses chlorinated cleaners (bleach-based sanitizers) or if the conveyor operates in a high-humidity coastal environment. 316 costs more than 304 because molybdenum and the extra nickel are expensive alloying elements.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Factor</th>
<th>304 SS</th>
<th>316 SS</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Chloride resistance</td>
<td>Moderate</td>
<td>Excellent</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Acid resistance</td>
<td>Good (mild acids)</td>
<td>Excellent (strong acids)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Pitting resistance</td>
<td>Moderate (PREN ~19)</td>
<td>High (PREN ~25)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cost drivers</td>
<td>Baseline food-grade stainless</td>
<td>Higher alloy cost (molybdenum, more nickel)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Best for</td>
<td>Bakery, snack, dry goods, confectionery</td>
<td>Meat, dairy, seafood, citrus, pickled foods</td>
</tr>
<tr>
