<td>Chlorinated, acidic, and alkaline cleaners</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>PREN = Pitting Resistance Equivalent Number, calculated as %Cr + 3.3 × %Mo + 16 × %N. The values shown are approximate, based on nominal composition. Higher values indicate better pitting corrosion resistance.</p>
<h2 id="belts-heading">Food-Contact Belt Materials</h2>
<p>The belt is the primary food-contact surface on most food conveyors. <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/part-177" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA 21 CFR 177</a> (indirect food additives: polymers) lists which polymers can be used in food-contact articles in the United States. EU Regulation 1935/2004 sets equivalent standards for European markets. <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">Belt material selection</a> depends on your product type, processing temperature, cleaning method, and required belt surface (smooth, textured, perforated, or cleated).</p>
<h3>Thermoplastic Polyurethane (TPU)</h3>
<p>TPU belts are the most common food-grade belt material. They resist oils, fats, and most cleaning chemicals. TPU provides good abrasion resistance at chilled-to-warm processing temperatures. Temperature limits vary by compound, so confirm them on the belt supplier's data sheet. TPU belts work well for bakery products, snack foods, confectionery, fruits, and vegetables.</p>
<h3>Silicone</h3>
<p>Silicone belts tolerate higher temperatures than TPU and provide excellent release properties for sticky or dough-based products. They cost more than TPU and wear faster under heavy abrasion. Silicone belts are the standard choice for bakery lines carrying raw dough, sticky candy, and products exiting ovens or cooling tunnels.</p>
<h3>Modular Plastic (Acetal / Polypropylene)</h3>
<p><a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">Modular plastic belts</a> use interlocking links made from acetal (POM) or polypropylene. These belts allow full disassembly for cleaning, because individual links snap out without tools. Temperature limits depend on the resin. For example, Intralox lists its <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-900/perforated-flat-top" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Series 900 Perforated Flat Top</a> at 34 to 220 F in polypropylene and 34 to 200 F in acetal. Modular plastic belts suit meat, poultry, and seafood processing where thorough daily sanitation is critical.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/food-nercon-sanitary-mattop-patties.webp" width="572" height="429" alt="Round patties ride in rows on a white modular plastic belt through a stainless steel conveyor frame." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Patties on a white modular plastic belt in a stainless steel sanitary conveyor. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.nerconconveyors.com/Conveyor/Conveyor-systems/Sanitary-Conveyor-systems" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nercon</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Stainless Steel Wire Mesh</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belts use woven or welded 304/316 stainless steel wire. They handle far higher temperatures than plastic belts (the limit depends on the alloy), provide full drainage, and allow airflow for cooling or drying applications. Wire mesh belts are standard in baking ovens, frying systems, cooling tunnels, and spiral freezers.</p>
<h2 id="ip-heading">IP Ratings for Food Conveyor Components</h2>
<p>IP (Ingress Protection) ratings define how well motors, drives, sensors, and electrical enclosures resist water and dust penetration. Food plant environments range from dry packaging rooms (IP54 sufficient) to full high-pressure washdown zones (IP69K required). Specifying the wrong IP rating leads to motor failures, electrical shorts, and unplanned downtime.</p>
<p>Every motor, gearbox, photo-eye, and control enclosure on the conveyor needs an IP rating appropriate for its zone. The conveyor frame itself is not IP-rated. IP ratings apply to electrical and mechanical components.</p>
