<h2 id="mfg-heading">How to Evaluate Food Conveyor Options</h2>
<p>Food conveyor suppliers range from standard sanitary platform builders to custom fabricators and full-line integrators. Compare them on documented facts that match your sanitation program.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Certification for the exact configuration:</strong> USDA acceptance, 3-A authorization, and BISSC certification apply to specific models and designs. Ask for the document, not a brochure claim.</li>
<li><strong>Product-contact materials and finish:</strong> stainless grade (304 or 316), surface roughness, weld finish, and the FDA compliance basis for the belt.</li>
<li><strong>Electrical ratings:</strong> the IP rating of every motor, gearbox, sensor, and enclosure, matched to your cleaning pressure and temperature.</li>
<li><strong>Sanitation time:</strong> how long it takes to strip the conveyor for cleaning, and whether tools are needed. Watch a demonstration if you can.</li>
<li><strong>Harborage review:</strong> open frame or hollow tube, exposed threads, bearing placement relative to the food zone, and drainability.</li>
<li><strong>Line integration:</strong> whether the supplier quotes transfers, controls, and connections to upstream and downstream machines, or only the conveyor.</li>
</ul>
<p>See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">manufacturer directory</a> for supplier listings.</p>
<h2 id="cost-heading">What Drives Food Conveyor Costs</h2>
<p>Food-grade conveyors carry a significant cost premium over standard industrial conveyors. Every component must meet food safety requirements, which eliminates lower-cost material options available in non-food applications. The following factors have the largest impact on food conveyor pricing.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Frame material:</strong> 304 stainless steel costs much more than painted carbon steel, and 316 costs more than 304.</li>
<li><strong>Belt material:</strong> FDA-compliant TPU and modular plastic belts cost more than general-purpose PVC or rubber belting.</li>
<li><strong>Motors and drives:</strong> IP69K washdown-rated stainless steel motors cost several times more than standard TEFC motors. Stainless steel gearboxes add further cost.</li>
<li><strong>Bearings:</strong> Sealed bearings with food-grade lubricant (meeting <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/178.3570" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 178.3570</a> for incidental food contact) cost more than standard bearings.</li>
<li><strong>Fasteners:</strong> All-stainless hardware with no exposed threads or recesses adds material and labor cost.</li>
<li><strong>Design complexity:</strong> Open-frame designs, tip-up tails, tool-free belt removal, and self-draining surfaces add engineering and fabrication cost.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> provides detailed pricing factor breakdowns across all conveyor types, including food-grade configurations.</p>
