<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What makes a conveyor food-grade?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A food-grade conveyor uses materials and construction methods that meet FDA, USDA, 3-A SSI, or BISSC sanitary standards. Key requirements include 304 or 316 stainless steel frames with smooth welds, FDA-compliant belt materials, sealed bearings, and tool-free disassembly for cleaning. Motors, sensors, and enclosures carry IP ratings matched to the cleaning method, and the design eliminates harborage points where bacteria can grow.</p>
<p>Read our <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary conveyor guide</a> for detailed design criteria.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the difference between 304 and 316 stainless steel for food conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><strong>304 stainless steel</strong> is the standard grade for most food conveyor applications. It handles water, mild cleaning chemicals, and most food products.</p>
<p><strong>316 stainless steel</strong> adds about 2-3% molybdenum for better chloride pitting resistance. Choose 316 for salt-heavy or acidic products and for facilities using chlorine-based sanitizers. 316 costs more than 304 because of its molybdenum and higher nickel content.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What IP rating do I need for a food conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>IP65 (6.3 mm nozzle, 12.5 L/min water jets) suits splash zones and light cleaning. IP66 (12.5 mm nozzle, 100 L/min) suits routine hose-down. <strong>IP69K</strong> is tested with 80 C (176 F) water at 80-100 bar (about 1,160-1,450 psi) from 10-15 cm and suits high-pressure, hot-water washdown common in meat, poultry, and dairy plants.</p>
<p>Neither USDA nor 3-A mandates a specific IP rating. Match the rating to your actual sanitation procedure.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How much more do food-grade conveyors cost?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Food-grade conveyors cost substantially more than equivalent painted carbon steel conveyors. The premium covers stainless steel construction, FDA-compliant belts, sealed bearings, washdown-rated motors, and sanitary design features like tip-up tails and open frames.</p>
<p>See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for detailed pricing factors.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">How should I compare food-grade conveyor suppliers?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Ask each supplier for documentation, not claims: the specific USDA, 3-A, or BISSC certification for the exact model and configuration quoted, the stainless grade and surface finish of product-contact parts, the FDA compliance basis for the belt, the IP rating of every motor and sensor, and the time and tools needed to strip the conveyor for sanitation. Compare quotes against the same cleaning procedure and chemicals.</p>

</div>
</div>
